<LessonLayout title="Catechist Guide &mdash; Jesus Christ, God and Man" cic="CCC 422-483" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Catechist Guide</h2>
<p class="big-question">For preparing and leading the class. For catechist use only.</p>

<h3>Class Summary</h3>

<p><strong>Estimated Duration:</strong> 75&ndash;90 minutes</p>
<p><strong>Central Theme:</strong> Jesus of Nazareth is true God and true man; in the union of His two natures (hypostatic union) lies the mystery of our salvation.</p>
<p><strong>Objectives:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Recognize Jesus as the eternal Son of God, "begotten, not made, consubstantial with the Father" (Nicaea, CCC 464&ndash;465).</li>
  <li>Understand the full humanity of Christ: "like us in all things but sin" (CCC 470&ndash;471).</li>
  <li>Learn the definition of Chalcedon on the hypostatic union and its importance for the faith (CCC 467).</li>
  <li>Discover the meaning of kenosis as key to the Incarnation and a model for Christian life (CCC 471&ndash;474).</li>
  <li>Learn to live ordinary life (work, family, routine) as a place of encounter with Christ, in light of the thirty years in Nazareth.</li>
  <li>Respond to Jesus' question: "But you, who do you say that I am?" with a personal, committed confession.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key Verse:</strong> "And the Word became flesh and dwelt among us" (Jn 1:14)</p>
<p><strong>Catechism References:</strong> CCC 422&ndash;483</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Suggested Class Structure</h3>

<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.85rem;margin:16px 0">
<tr style="background:var(--gold-light)">
<th style="padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid var(--border)">Section</th>
<th style="padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid var(--border)">Time</th>
<th style="padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid var(--border)">What the Catechist Does</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">1. The Great Question: "Who do you say I am?"</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">10 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Read Mk 8:27&ndash;30. Ask: "Who do people today say Jesus is?" (typical answers: great teacher, prophet, revolutionary, myth...). Then ask the personal question: "And you, who do you say He is?" Listen without judging. If someone says "I don't know," validate it: "That's an excellent starting point."</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">2. Jesus: The Name That Says It All</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">10 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Explain the meaning of "Jesus" (God saves), "Christ" (Anointed One), and other titles: Lord, Son of God, Word, Emmanuel. Activity: have each participant research the meaning of their own name and share.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">3. True God: Nicaea and the Battle for Divinity</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">15 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Read Jn 1:1, 14. Tell the story of Arius and Nicaea: why does it matter that Jesus is God, not just the most perfect creature? Present C.S. Lewis's trilemma (madman, liar, or Lord). Emphasize: if Jesus is not God, He cannot save us.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">4. True Man: Chalcedon and the Hypostatic Union</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">12 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Read Heb 4:15. Explain Jesus' real humanity: why do we need Him to be man? The formula of Chalcedon (without confusion, without change, without division, without separation). Use the coin analogy: two sides, one coin.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">5. Kenosis: God Emptied Himself for Love</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">12 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Read Phil 2:6&ndash;11 slowly. Explain kenosis as the key to divine love and a model for Christian life. Ask: what does kenosis mean for daily life? Connect with the Annunciation and Mary's fiat.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">6. Nazareth: Thirty Years of Ordinary Life</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">10 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Read Lk 2:51&ndash;52. Reflect: God spent most of His earthly life in total ordinariness. What does this tell us about the value of our ordinary lives? Brief activity: "One thing from your daily routine you would do differently."</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">7. Final Reflection and Prayer</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">6 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Review the Key Idea. Invite each person to write or silently speak their response to Jesus' question. Close with the prayer from the workbook.</td>
</tr>
</table>

