<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>C.S. Lewis's Trilemma as a Pastoral Tool</h4>
<p>Lewis's trilemma (madman, liar, or Lord) is one of the most effective apologetic tools for young people and adults who reduce Jesus to a "great moral teacher." The force of the argument is simple: someone who claims to be God cannot be merely a good teacher. He is either lying, insane, or who He says He is. The catechist's question should be: "Which option seems most reasonable, given the Gospels?" This isn't about "winning" an argument but about leading the person to the threshold of faith. Faith is a gift, but reason prepares the way.</p>

<h4>Nicaea and Chalcedon: Why Conciliar Christology Matters</h4>
<p>The first four Ecumenical Councils (Nicaea 325, Constantinople 381, Ephesus 431, Chalcedon 451) are the Church's response to Christological errors that, if accepted, would destroy the faith. Each council answers a specific question: (1) Is Jesus true God? Yes, consubstantial with the Father (Nicaea). (2) Is the Holy Spirit true God? Yes (Constantinople). (3) Is Mary Mother of God? Yes, because the One born of her is God (Ephesus). (4) How are divinity and humanity united in Christ? In one Person, two natures, without confusion, without change, without division, without separation (Chalcedon).</p>
<p>A useful teaching aid: draw the heresies as deviations from orthodoxy. Docetism says Jesus only appeared human (too divine). Arianism says Jesus is not true God (too human). Nestorianism separates the divine and human so much that there are practically two persons. Monophysitism mixes the natures into one. Orthodoxy is the balance defined by Chalcedon: two natures, one Person.</p>

<h4>Kenosis as the Key to Everyday Holiness</h4>
<p>Kenosis is not just a theological concept: it is the heart of Christian spirituality. St. Paul says: "Have this mind among yourselves, which is yours in Christ Jesus" (Phil 2:5), and then develops the kenosis hymn. The imitation of Christ begins here: emptying oneself of ego, pride, need for control, and the pretense of always being right. Emptying so that God may fill. Dying to oneself so that Christ may live in us. This is the dynamic of holiness: not a conquest, but a stripping away.</p>
<p>The catechist can ask: "Is there something in your life you need to let go of so that God has space?" The question must be concrete, not generic.</p>

<h4>The Hidden Life of Nazareth: Recovering the Holiness of the Ordinary</h4>
<p>One of the most striking aspects of this class is the fact of the thirty years in Nazareth. Many people live with the feeling that their "normal" life is of no interest to God, that what is truly spiritual happens somewhere else (a retreat, a convent, a mission). Jesus refutes this with thirty years of workshop, saw, sawdust, difficult customers, and family meals. God chose to live like this. Therefore holiness is not elsewhere: it hides in the workday, in the family meal, in daily duties.</p>
<p>St. Josemar&iacute;a Escriv&aacute; preached this forcefully: "God waits for you in the ordinary." The class can close with a very concrete invitation: this week, live your routine as if God were at your side &mdash; because He is.</p>

<h4>Connecting with Incarnational Spirituality</h4>
<p>The Incarnation is not only a historical event two thousand years ago. It is the permanent shape of God's relationship with the world. God continues to come to us through material things: the sacraments (water, bread, wine, oil), the Church as visible community, the poor in whom Christ is present. An incarnational spirituality does not flee the world but transforms it from within. The catechist can connect this with the theology of St. John Paul II (Theology of the Body) and with the social doctrine of the Church: if God became flesh, the material world matters. The body matters. Social justice matters. The Incarnation is the foundation of a Christian humanism that takes the world seriously.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Adaptation by Age Group</h3>

<h4>&#127793; Seed (Children 7&ndash;12)</h4>
<p>Use the video game analogy: "If the game's creator appears as a character inside the game, what would happen?" Explain that Jesus is God who entered His own creation. The coin: two sides, one coin = two natures, one Person. Activity: draw the manger scene with a golden circle of light (divinity) surrounding the child (humanity). Memorize Jn 1:14 with gestures. Teach the Sign of the Cross and explain that Jesus' name is so holy we bow our heads when saying it.</p>

<h4>&#127807; Shoots (Teens 13&ndash;17)</h4>
<p>Connect with the search for identity characteristic of adolescence. "Who are you?" is the question teens ask daily. "Who is Jesus?" is the question God asks back. Lewis's trilemma is very effective with teens; it forces them to take a position. Use kenosis as counter-culture: in a world that says "be yourself, never lower yourself, never back down," Jesus says "whoever wants to be first must be servant of all." The life of Bl. Carlo Acutis, a millennial teen who used his tech skills to spread Eucharistic devotion, resonates powerfully here.</p>

<h4>&#127806; Root (Adults New to the Faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults approach faith with a "low Christology": Jesus as ethical model, friend, inspiration. The challenge is helping them make the step to full Christological faith: Jesus is not just a model to follow, but God who comes to save us. Moving from "admiring Jesus" to "entrusting life to Jesus" is the step of the catechumenate. The testimony of famous converts (Lewis, Chesterton, St. Augustine) can be a great help.</p>

<h4>&#127794; Tree (Formed Adults)</h4>
<p>Deepen with patristic theology. St. Athanasius: "God became man so that man might become God" (<em>De Incarnatione</em>, 54). St. Irenaeus: "The Word of God became what we are to make us what He is" (<em>Adversus Haereses</em>, V). St. Thomas Aquinas: STh III, qq.1&ndash;15 on the fittingness of the Incarnation. Connect with 20th-century theology: Karl Rahner and "anonymous Christianity"; Hans Urs von Balthasar and Trinitarian kenosis. John Paul II's encyclical <em>Redemptor Hominis</em> is a fundamental text.</p>

</section>

