<section class="content">

<h3>Tough Questions</h3>

<h4>"Why did God become man? Couldn't He have saved us some other, easier way?"</h4>
<p>St. Thomas Aquinas (STh III, q.1, a.2) and St. Anselm (<em>Cur Deus Homo</em>) respond: God, being omnipotent, could have saved us in many ways. He chose this one because it was the most fitting for manifesting His love and His justice. The Incarnation is not the only possible form of salvation, but it is the most beautiful and the most worthy of God: it saves us from the inside, sharing our condition, not from outside as an impersonal decree. Moreover, only a human being could represent humanity and offer a proportionate reparation; only God could offer an infinite reparation. Jesus is the only one who fulfills both conditions (CCC 459&ndash;460).</p>

<h4>"How can Jesus be 100% God and 100% man? That adds up to 200%."</h4>
<p>The coin analogy (two sides, one coin) is visually helpful but limited. Better is the analogy of the Person: divinity and humanity in Christ are not added like quantities; they are united in a single Person. It's like the relationship between soul and body in us: we are not "50% soul and 50% body"; we are one person with two dimensions. In Christ, the Person is divine (the Word), and this divine Person assumes a complete human nature. There is no mathematical addition: there is an ontological union in one hypostasis (CCC 467&ndash;468).</p>

<h4>"If Jesus was God, why did He pray to the Father? Why didn't He know the day of judgment?"</h4>
<p>Jesus possessed two kinds of knowledge: divine knowledge (proper to His divine nature, omniscient) and human knowledge (limited, progressive). In His humanity, He experienced real growth: "Jesus increased in wisdom" (Lk 2:52). Catholic theology speaks of three types of human knowledge in Christ: the beatific vision (immediate knowledge of God), infused knowledge (supernatural knowledge given by God, like the gift of prophecy), and acquired knowledge (normal human knowledge through experience and learning). When Jesus says He doesn't know the day of judgment (Mk 13:32), He is speaking from His human knowledge, which was limited as part of His kenosis. This doesn't contradict His divinity: God freely chooses to submit Himself to human conditions in order to save us from within (CCC 472&ndash;474).</p>

<h4>"Why does the virginal birth matter? Isn't it just a legend?"</h4>
<p>The virginal conception of Jesus is affirmed in both infancy Gospels (Mt 1:18&ndash;25; Lk 1:26&ndash;38) and in the Creed. Its theological meaning is profound: (1) It manifests that the initiative of the Incarnation is entirely divine, not the fruit of human history. (2) It shows that Jesus is Son of God in the strict sense, not of Joseph. (3) It fulfills the prophecy of Isaiah 7:14: "The virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel." Denying the virginal birth in practice usually leads to a lowered Christology where Jesus is only an exceptional man. The Church believes Jesus was conceived by the power of the Holy Spirit in the womb of the Virgin Mary, "without the agency of a man" (CCC 496&ndash;498, 502&ndash;507).</p>

<h4>"Is Lewis's trilemma too simplistic? What if the Gospels exaggerated?"</h4>
<p>It's true that the trilemma presupposes the historical reliability of the Gospels. If someone objects, "Jesus never claimed to be God; the Church invented that later," the response has two levels: (1) Historical: the Gospels are reliable sources, and Paul's letters (50s&ndash;60s AD) already confess Christ's divinity. The gap between Jesus' death (c. 30 AD) and the earliest writings is only twenty years, insufficient for a myth to develop. (2) Existential: Jesus, as all available sources present Him, behaved as someone with divine authority: He forgave sins (something only God can do), claimed to be greater than the Temple and the Sabbath, and accepted being called "My Lord and my God" (Thomas, Jn 20:28). Either all of that is false, or it is true. There is no middle ground, and the available evidence points strongly toward its truth.</p>

<h4>"Did Jesus know He was God from childhood?"</h4>
<p>Catholic theology responds that yes, from the first instant of His conception, Jesus had full consciousness of His divine identity (through the beatific vision). But this does not eliminate human development: Luke says He "increased in wisdom" (Lk 2:52). Jesus' human consciousness was real and gradual in its expression, although He possessed His divine identity from all eternity. It's somewhat analogous to how a child prodigy "knows" things intrinsically but articulates them progressively (with all the cautions of analogy). The ultimate mystery remains, and it is proper to the kenosis: God chooses to live the human condition in all its dimensions, including psychological growth (CCC 470&ndash;474).</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus,<br />
Eternal Word of the Father, true God and true man,<br />
You who emptied Your glory to find me<br />
in my flesh, in my time, in my small story,<br />
teach me to speak of You with the truth of the Creed<br />
but also with the fire of love.<br />
May those who listen not remain in ideas about You<br />
but encounter You, the living Lord.<br />
May my catechesis not be mere human word<br />
but an echo of the Word who became flesh.<br />
And at the end of the class,<br />
may each person be able to answer, like Peter,<br />
not with memorized words<br />
but with a heart on fire:<br />
"You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

